1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:01,032
Night.

2
00:00:01,106 --> 00:00:02,582
Three hundred thousand years ago.

3
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:04,801
Fardin is hiding behind a rock.

4
00:00:04,831 --> 00:00:05,920
And he is not alone.

5
00:00:06,176 --> 00:00:07,615
Something is circling the camp.

6
00:00:07,705 --> 00:00:08,905
Then comes the sound.

7
00:00:10,851 --> 00:00:12,025
Spotted hyenas.

8
00:00:12,045 --> 00:00:14,288
And Fardin just realized something terrible.

9
00:00:14,566 --> 00:00:16,601
The people around this fire can't see

10
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:16,999
him.

11
00:00:17,159 --> 00:00:19,562
But he has no idea if the hyenas can.

12
00:00:20,977 --> 00:00:22,762
But to understand how he got here,

13
00:00:22,782 --> 00:00:24,480
we need to go back a few hours.

14
00:00:25,347 --> 00:00:26,473
This is Fardin.

15
00:00:26,678 --> 00:00:27,538
Normal guy.

16
00:00:27,598 --> 00:00:28,180
Red hoodie.

17
00:00:28,246 --> 00:00:29,296
One very unusual watch.

18
00:00:29,457 --> 00:00:31,467
Turn the dial, and he falls back through

19
00:00:31,487 --> 00:00:32,415
time as a ghost.

20
00:00:32,636 --> 00:00:34,766
Nobody there can see him or hear him.

21
00:00:34,876 --> 00:00:35,986
He can only watch.

22
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:37,856
Tonight, he set the dial further back

23
00:00:37,876 --> 00:00:38,455
than ever.

24
00:00:39,958 --> 00:00:41,277
Three hundred thousand years.

25
00:00:45,355 --> 00:00:47,065
In the next twenty four hours,

26
00:00:47,085 --> 00:00:48,924
he'll see three things that changed

27
00:00:48,944 --> 00:00:50,757
how we think about our species.

28
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:52,606
A hunt with stone tipped spears.

29
00:00:52,827 --> 00:00:54,251
A rock trick so clever,

30
00:00:54,271 --> 00:00:56,309
it lasted two hundred thousand years.

31
00:00:56,513 --> 00:00:58,695
And by tonight, the strangest discovery

32
00:00:58,715 --> 00:01:00,575
of all: who else was out there.

33
00:01:01,636 --> 00:01:02,064
Dawn.

34
00:01:02,218 --> 00:01:03,920
Fardin is standing on a dry,

35
00:01:03,940 --> 00:01:06,072
rocky hillside, in what will one day

36
00:01:06,092 --> 00:01:06,849
be Morocco.

37
00:01:07,099 --> 00:01:08,600
There are no countries yet,

38
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:10,543
no farms, and no cities anywhere on

39
00:01:10,563 --> 00:01:11,017
Earth.

40
00:01:11,286 --> 00:01:13,366
Scientists call this place Jebel Irhoud.

41
00:01:13,601 --> 00:01:16,000
In 2017, researchers announced that

42
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:18,658
human fossils found here are about three

43
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:20,560
hundred thousand years old.

44
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:22,778
They are the oldest known fossils of

45
00:01:22,798 --> 00:01:23,806
our own species.

46
00:01:23,926 --> 00:01:25,021
Homo sapiens.

47
00:01:25,784 --> 00:01:26,587
And there they are.

48
00:01:26,607 --> 00:01:28,818
A small group, waking up beside a cave.

49
00:01:29,096 --> 00:01:30,667
Fardin's first thought?

50
00:01:30,687 --> 00:01:31,152
They look...

51
00:01:31,174 --> 00:01:31,927
surprisingly normal.

52
00:01:32,325 --> 00:01:34,293
And he's right, mostly.

53
00:01:34,389 --> 00:01:35,985
Their faces were a lot like ours.

54
00:01:36,005 --> 00:01:37,782
But the back of the skull was longer

55
00:01:37,802 --> 00:01:38,433
and lower.

56
00:01:38,704 --> 00:01:40,826
Their brains were about as big as ours,

57
00:01:40,846 --> 00:01:42,137
but shaped differently.

58
00:01:42,347 --> 00:01:44,220
The rounder brain shape we have today

59
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:45,827
seems to have appeared later.

60
00:01:46,645 --> 00:01:48,442
Here's a way to feel how long ago this

61
00:01:48,462 --> 00:01:48,762
is.

62
00:01:48,992 --> 00:01:51,285
Squeeze the whole story of our species

63
00:01:51,305 --> 00:01:53,251
into one twenty four hour day.

64
00:01:53,511 --> 00:01:55,710
Farming doesn't show up until about

65
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:56,522
eleven PM.

66
00:01:56,767 --> 00:01:57,538
The pyramids?

67
00:01:57,655 --> 00:01:58,760
Eleven thirty eight.

68
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:00,879
Almost everything in your history books

69
00:02:00,899 --> 00:02:02,621
happens in the last half hour.

70
00:02:03,415 --> 00:02:05,175
And forget the Sahara you know.

71
00:02:05,315 --> 00:02:07,650
At times, North Africa was much greener,

72
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:09,689
with grasslands, lakes and rivers.

73
00:02:09,904 --> 00:02:12,318
Gazelles and other grazing animals roamed

74
00:02:12,338 --> 00:02:12,786
here.

75
00:02:12,976 --> 00:02:15,004
Fardin tries waving at a man walking

76
00:02:15,024 --> 00:02:16,008
right past him.

77
00:02:17,416 --> 00:02:17,866
Nothing.

78
00:02:17,928 --> 00:02:18,747
Not even a blink.

79
00:02:18,777 --> 00:02:20,069
He really is a ghost here.

80
00:02:20,315 --> 00:02:21,200
But that's fine.

81
00:02:21,317 --> 00:02:23,183
Because the hunt is about to begin.

82
00:02:23,283 --> 00:02:25,334
And it's nothing compared to what happens

83
00:02:25,354 --> 00:02:26,062
after dark.

84
00:02:26,953 --> 00:02:27,731
Mid morning.

85
00:02:27,751 --> 00:02:29,726
A few hunters grab their spears and

86
00:02:29,746 --> 00:02:31,747
head out, and Fardin tags along,

87
00:02:31,767 --> 00:02:32,952
three steps behind.

88
00:02:33,183 --> 00:02:35,944
Archaeologists dug up many animal bones

89
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:38,314
at Jebel Irhoud, and the most common

90
00:02:38,334 --> 00:02:40,076
by far belonged to gazelles.

91
00:02:40,316 --> 00:02:42,758
Many carry cut marks from stone tools.

92
00:02:43,557 --> 00:02:44,622
Here's the problem.

93
00:02:44,754 --> 00:02:46,658
A gazelle can sprint at up to around

94
00:02:46,678 --> 00:02:48,142
eighty kilometers an hour.

95
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:49,873
No human can catch one.

96
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:51,669
So the hunters creep in low.

97
00:02:51,709 --> 00:02:52,108
Quiet.

98
00:02:52,188 --> 00:02:52,702
Downwind.

99
00:02:52,782 --> 00:02:53,219
Closer.

100
00:02:53,509 --> 00:02:54,321
And closer.

101
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:56,124
Now!

102
00:02:57,018 --> 00:02:59,154
The spear flies, and misses.

103
00:02:59,182 --> 00:03:00,270
The whole herd explodes away.

104
00:03:00,564 --> 00:03:02,832
Fardin is laughing so hard he forgets

105
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:03,829
he's invisible.

106
00:03:04,009 --> 00:03:06,474
Then he remembers, and laughs even louder.

107
00:03:08,123 --> 00:03:09,481
But the hunters don't give up.

108
00:03:09,501 --> 00:03:10,451
They spread out.

109
00:03:10,581 --> 00:03:11,103
Patient.

110
00:03:11,215 --> 00:03:12,239
Working as a team.

111
00:03:12,352 --> 00:03:14,015
Hunting like this takes planning,

112
00:03:14,035 --> 00:03:14,666
and trust.

113
00:03:14,897 --> 00:03:16,867
On the next try, one throw finds its

114
00:03:16,887 --> 00:03:17,292
mark.

115
00:03:17,889 --> 00:03:19,869
Some stone points from this site look

116
00:03:19,889 --> 00:03:21,846
like they were made to be fixed onto

117
00:03:21,866 --> 00:03:22,739
wooden shafts.

118
00:03:22,919 --> 00:03:25,619
Did these hunters throw their spears,

119
00:03:25,639 --> 00:03:26,169
or stab up close?

120
00:03:26,239 --> 00:03:28,182
Scientists are still debating that.

121
00:03:28,461 --> 00:03:30,084
And the strangest part of their day

122
00:03:30,104 --> 00:03:31,108
hasn't happened yet.

123
00:03:31,988 --> 00:03:33,670
Back at camp, it's lunchtime.

124
00:03:33,690 --> 00:03:35,495
And Fardin learns a rule fast.

125
00:03:35,646 --> 00:03:36,874
Nothing gets wasted.

126
00:03:37,099 --> 00:03:39,113
They slice the meat with stone flakes,

127
00:03:39,133 --> 00:03:40,913
sharp enough to cut through hide.

128
00:03:41,697 --> 00:03:43,610
Then someone grabs a heavy rock,

129
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:45,727
places a leg bone on a flat stone,

130
00:03:45,747 --> 00:03:47,033
and smashes it open.

131
00:03:47,272 --> 00:03:48,258
Inside is marrow.

132
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,293
Soft, fatty, and full of energy.

133
00:03:50,373 --> 00:03:51,983
They scoop it out, and eat it.

134
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:52,218
Raw.

135
00:03:52,609 --> 00:03:54,435
Fardin looks like he's about to be sick.

136
00:03:56,138 --> 00:03:57,896
But to them, this is a treat.

137
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:00,419
Humans and our ancestors had been cracking

138
00:04:00,439 --> 00:04:02,714
bones for marrow for more than a million

139
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:03,216
years.

140
00:04:03,973 --> 00:04:05,156
Did they cook any of it?

141
00:04:05,216 --> 00:04:05,996
Possibly.

142
00:04:06,146 --> 00:04:08,055
Some flint tools here were burned in

143
00:04:08,075 --> 00:04:10,198
fire, and that's actually how scientists

144
00:04:10,218 --> 00:04:11,153
dated the site.

145
00:04:11,393 --> 00:04:13,380
But how often people cooked their food

146
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:15,250
this long ago is still uncertain.

147
00:04:15,571 --> 00:04:17,888
They almost certainly ate plants too.

148
00:04:17,908 --> 00:04:19,818
Roots, seeds and fruits.

149
00:04:19,978 --> 00:04:21,987
But plants rarely survive this long,

150
00:04:22,007 --> 00:04:23,679
so most of the menu is lost.

151
00:04:23,911 --> 00:04:25,475
Fardin decides to skip lunch.

152
00:04:25,736 --> 00:04:26,399
Good call.

153
00:04:26,419 --> 00:04:27,558
Because later tonight,

154
00:04:27,578 --> 00:04:29,286
he'll find out these people were not

155
00:04:29,306 --> 00:04:30,462
alone on this planet.

156
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:31,969
Afternoon.

157
00:04:32,099 --> 00:04:33,569
In the shade of the cliff,

158
00:04:33,589 --> 00:04:35,742
an older man sits down with two stones.

159
00:04:35,962 --> 00:04:37,674
Fardin sits right next to him.

160
00:04:37,918 --> 00:04:38,966
This, it turns out,

161
00:04:38,986 --> 00:04:39,978
is high technology.

162
00:04:40,241 --> 00:04:41,906
He doesn't just smash the rock.

163
00:04:42,136 --> 00:04:43,953
He shapes it, chip by chip,

164
00:04:43,973 --> 00:04:45,986
as if he's planning several moves ahead.

165
00:04:46,076 --> 00:04:48,012
Every strike has to land at just the

166
00:04:48,032 --> 00:04:48,802
right angle.

167
00:04:49,606 --> 00:04:51,046
Then, one precise strike...

168
00:04:52,068 --> 00:04:53,740
and a ready made blade pops off,

169
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,190
sharp along its edges.

170
00:04:55,398 --> 00:04:57,680
Archaeologists call this the Levallois

171
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:58,432
technique.

172
00:04:58,662 --> 00:05:00,762
The toolmaker had to picture the final

173
00:05:00,782 --> 00:05:02,109
tool inside the stone,

174
00:05:02,129 --> 00:05:03,252
before it existed.

175
00:05:03,531 --> 00:05:05,870
Methods like this were used across Africa

176
00:05:05,890 --> 00:05:07,971
and beyond for more than two hundred

177
00:05:07,991 --> 00:05:08,971
thousand years.

178
00:05:09,746 --> 00:05:11,818
Fire is part of life here too.

179
00:05:11,838 --> 00:05:14,093
Burnt flint and bone tell us these people

180
00:05:14,113 --> 00:05:14,708
used it.

181
00:05:14,978 --> 00:05:16,937
Fire meant warmth, light,

182
00:05:16,957 --> 00:05:18,560
protection, and maybe cooked food.

183
00:05:18,827 --> 00:05:20,317
And far away in Kenya,

184
00:05:20,337 --> 00:05:22,327
at a site called Olorgesailie,

185
00:05:22,347 --> 00:05:24,788
people from around this same time carried

186
00:05:24,808 --> 00:05:27,277
black obsidian rock from as far as ninety

187
00:05:27,297 --> 00:05:28,366
kilometers away.

188
00:05:28,651 --> 00:05:31,284
Ninety kilometers is roughly two marathons,

189
00:05:31,304 --> 00:05:31,942
on foot.

190
00:05:32,122 --> 00:05:34,692
They also collected red and black pigments.

191
00:05:34,842 --> 00:05:36,412
Was that trade between groups?

192
00:05:36,432 --> 00:05:38,052
Some scientists think so.

193
00:05:38,072 --> 00:05:39,198
Others aren't sure.

194
00:05:40,005 --> 00:05:42,043
Either way, these were people who planned

195
00:05:42,063 --> 00:05:43,772
ahead, remembered and connected.

196
00:05:43,932 --> 00:05:45,631
Just with very different gear.

197
00:05:45,894 --> 00:05:47,524
Then the sun starts to sink.

198
00:05:47,714 --> 00:05:49,830
And Fardin is about to find out why

199
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:52,029
nobody here wanders off after dark.

200
00:05:52,938 --> 00:05:53,520
Sunset.

201
00:05:53,540 --> 00:05:55,240
The group pulls in close around the

202
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:55,650
fire.

203
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:57,542
Fardin finally relaxes.

204
00:05:57,754 --> 00:05:58,599
Big mistake.

205
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:01,589
Out in the dark, shapes are moving.

206
00:06:01,835 --> 00:06:04,132
Across Africa, early humans shared the

207
00:06:04,152 --> 00:06:06,502
land with lions, leopards and spotted

208
00:06:06,522 --> 00:06:07,116
hyenas.

209
00:06:07,336 --> 00:06:08,178
And here we are.

210
00:06:08,198 --> 00:06:09,950
This is the moment from the beginning.

211
00:06:10,735 --> 00:06:12,265
The hyenas circle closer.

212
00:06:12,355 --> 00:06:14,236
The adults rise, spears ready.

213
00:06:14,494 --> 00:06:16,067
Someone grabs a burning branch,

214
00:06:16,087 --> 00:06:17,633
and hurls it toward the dark.

215
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:19,201
The eyes back away.

216
00:06:19,632 --> 00:06:21,547
Fire probably helped keep predators

217
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:22,496
at a distance.

218
00:06:22,636 --> 00:06:24,711
It's one of the oldest safety tricks

219
00:06:24,731 --> 00:06:25,810
our species has.

220
00:06:26,617 --> 00:06:28,253
Fardin's heart is pounding.

221
00:06:28,393 --> 00:06:30,333
Then he remembers, he's the only one

222
00:06:30,353 --> 00:06:32,175
here who can literally walk through

223
00:06:32,195 --> 00:06:32,723
a wall.

224
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:34,475
He's still not going out there.

225
00:06:36,319 --> 00:06:37,988
Late at night, inside the cave,

226
00:06:38,008 --> 00:06:40,298
people lie down on beds of grass.

227
00:06:40,448 --> 00:06:41,628
Fardin tries one.

228
00:06:41,648 --> 00:06:43,127
Surprisingly comfy.

229
00:06:43,391 --> 00:06:44,753
This part isn't a guess.

230
00:06:44,873 --> 00:06:46,827
At Border Cave in South Africa,

231
00:06:46,847 --> 00:06:48,933
scientists found grass bedding about

232
00:06:48,953 --> 00:06:50,890
two hundred thousand years old.

233
00:06:51,129 --> 00:06:52,722
It was laid on top of ash,

234
00:06:52,742 --> 00:06:54,892
possibly to keep crawling insects away.

235
00:06:55,704 --> 00:06:57,940
Fardin lies awake, staring out at the

236
00:06:57,960 --> 00:06:58,447
stars.

237
00:06:58,547 --> 00:06:59,849
Then his watch buzzes,

238
00:06:59,869 --> 00:07:00,997
with one last fact.

239
00:07:01,017 --> 00:07:02,060
The biggest one.

240
00:07:02,470 --> 00:07:04,313
These people were not the only humans

241
00:07:04,333 --> 00:07:04,932
on Earth.

242
00:07:06,592 --> 00:07:09,034
Around this time, there were Neanderthal

243
00:07:09,054 --> 00:07:10,346
ancestors in Europe,

244
00:07:10,366 --> 00:07:12,470
and mysterious Denisovans in Asia,

245
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:14,461
known mostly from their DNA.

246
00:07:14,735 --> 00:07:17,149
A small brained species called Homo

247
00:07:17,169 --> 00:07:19,286
naledi lived in South Africa.

248
00:07:19,556 --> 00:07:21,618
Another kind of archaic human lived

249
00:07:21,638 --> 00:07:23,096
in what is now Zambia.

250
00:07:23,246 --> 00:07:24,927
And on the island of Java,

251
00:07:24,947 --> 00:07:27,356
probably some of the last Homo erectus.

252
00:07:27,559 --> 00:07:29,440
At least five different kinds of humans

253
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:31,319
may have shared the planet at the same

254
00:07:31,339 --> 00:07:31,730
time.

255
00:07:32,514 --> 00:07:33,612
Today, only one is left.

256
00:07:33,632 --> 00:07:34,205
Us.

257
00:07:34,786 --> 00:07:37,106
Scientists are still working out why.

258
00:07:37,306 --> 00:07:39,519
Much later, some of those other humans

259
00:07:39,539 --> 00:07:41,696
even had children with our ancestors.

260
00:07:41,846 --> 00:07:44,342
That's why many people alive today carry

261
00:07:44,362 --> 00:07:46,882
a little Neanderthal or Denisovan DNA.

262
00:07:47,827 --> 00:07:48,799
Dawn is coming.

263
00:07:48,948 --> 00:07:50,920
Fardin's twenty four hours are almost

264
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:51,264
up.

265
00:07:51,494 --> 00:07:52,951
His watch starts to glow.

266
00:07:53,190 --> 00:07:54,872
But the dial is spinning the wrong way.

267
00:07:55,379 --> 00:07:58,240
Next time, Fardin lands in ancient Egypt,

268
00:07:58,330 --> 00:08:00,000
right in the middle of building the

269
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:00,812
Great Pyramid.

270
00:08:01,632 --> 00:08:04,080
Subscribe, or Fardin stays lost in time.

